{
  "title": "Ingredient extraction emits '+ ...' continuation fragments as \
separate ingredients",
  "description": "data/unique-ingredients.txt (the historical parsed \
ingredient corpus) contains 51 lines that start with '+ ', e.g. '+ 1 1/4 \
teaspoons salt', '+ 1 Tbsp warm water', '+ 1 tablespoon all-purpose flour'. \
These look like continuations of a preceding ingredient ('1 cup sugar + 1 \
tablespoon sugar' style, or a site listing an addition on its own line) that \
get extracted as standalone ingredients with a leading '+ '.\n\
\n\
Related smaller artifacts in the same corpus: 3 lines that are just \
parenthesized fragments or a bare ')' (e.g. '(440-gram) cans'), and 4 lines \
starting with '*' that are footnotes, not ingredients (e.g. '*Substitute \
octopus with sausage').\n\
\n\
Found while mining categorizer keyword gaps (these fragments mostly land in \
'Other' and no keyword list can fix them). Worth investigating where the split \
happens (extraction vs parsing) and either merging continuations into the \
prior ingredient, stripping the marker, or dropping footnote lines. Rerun \
`make pipeline` after any fix and check the data/ diffs.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
  "type": "task",
